# Anti-Spaghetti Guardrails

Always enforce these defaults unless the user explicitly overrides:

- Keep module boundaries clear: `routing` for intent/plan, `services` for business orchestration, `llm` for model I/O and validation, `tools` for tool adapters.
- Avoid god files: when a file grows beyond ~400 lines or mixes 3+ responsibilities, split it before adding more logic.
- Prefer extraction over duplication: if logic is repeated in two places, create a shared helper/module and replace both call sites.
- Preserve execution chain clarity: `API -> Router/Planner -> Executor -> Tools -> Response`.
- Do not add compatibility shims unless user asks; migrate imports to canonical modules directly.
- Before adding a new file, check if an existing focused module can own the change.
- For every substantial refactor, run full tests and keep behavior stable unless user explicitly allows contract changes.

## Refactor Checklist

- [ ] No new cross-layer imports that violate boundaries
- [ ] No repeated fallback/branch logic left in parallel modules
- [ ] Tests cover changed behavior and still pass
- [ ] Docs updated only when behavior/contract changes
